  "description": "Denoising is learned as a single-channel, zero-phase, frequency-domain Wiener filter estimated from the training set. For each segment we compute an rFFT; across all training segments we estimate, per frequency bin, the linear least-squares transfer H(f) that maps the mixture X(f) to the clean target Y(f): H = sum_i conj(X_i) Y_i / (sum_i |X_i|^2 + \u03bb). Because both mixture and clean are normalized by the mixture\u2019s per-segment standard deviation, the ratio is invariant to that scaling and approximates S_cc/(S_cc+S_aa). We constrain H to be real (zero-phase), clamp it to [0,1], and smooth it across frequency to reduce variance. Hyperparameters (\u03bb relative Tikhonov, smoothing window length, and a small shrinkage toward identity) are chosen by a small deterministic 80/20 hold-out split to minimize average per-segment relative RMSE on the validation fold. With the best hyperparameters, H is re-estimated on all training data and applied to test segments via rFFT -> multiply by H -> irFFT. The result has the same shape as test_x. This is computationally light, deterministic, and respects the per-segment standardization noted in the context.",
